  <div class="wrap">
<!-- ============ SECTION 1 ============ -->
  
  <div class="section-header" id="s1"><span class="num">1</span><h2>Conceptual Framework Linkage</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card ci">
      <div class="callout suc">WHERE THE SERIES COMES TOGETHER: §266 is the exact, legally prescribed line-item structure that Rückstellungen (§249), RAP (§250), goodwill (§246), and deferred taxes (§274) all slot directly into. This poster also confirms the current, EU-driven 2024 size thresholds now governing every size-dependent relief mentioned throughout this series.</div>

  
      <div class="ctitle ci">Where everything this series has built finally has a fixed address</div>
      <div class="principle">Every concept this poster series has covered – Rückstellungen, RAP, goodwill, deferred tax, equity components – now finds its EXACT, legally mandated location within a single, prescribed balance sheet format. §266 is not a suggestion or a common presentation convention; it is the LAW itself dictating, line by line, where each item must appear.</div>
      <div class="elabel">HOW §§266–274A MAP TO GERMAN ACCOUNTING PRINCIPLES</div>
      <div class="check-list">
        <span><strong>Bewertungsstetigkeit, applied to structure</strong> – the format itself, not merely measurement methods, must remain consistent year to year</span>
        <span><strong>Size-dependent proportionality</strong> – §267/§267a scale disclosure burden to genuine company size, a cost-benefit theme running throughout HGB</span>
        <span><strong>Gläubigerschutz through equity transparency</strong> – §272's detailed equity component rules exist specifically so creditors can see exactly how much genuine, distributable capital cushion actually exists</span>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="card cs">
      <div class="ctitle cs">What §§266–274a require, at a glance</div>
      <div class="td-table">
        <div class="th cols-06-17"><span>§</span><span>Core requirement</span></div>
        <div class="tr cols-06-17"><span>266</span><span>The prescribed Bilanz line-item structure, both sides</span></div>
        <div class="tr alt cols-06-17"><span>267–267a</span><span>Four size classes, driving disclosure/audit/filing relief throughout Book 3</span></div>
        <div class="tr cols-06-17"><span>268</span><span>Specific item rules – profit appropriation presentation, among others</span></div>
        <div class="tr alt cols-06-17"><span>270–271</span><span>Reserve formation rules; definitions of Beteiligung and Verbundene Unternehmen</span></div>
        <div class="tr cols-06-17"><span>272</span><span>Detailed equity component rules, including treasury shares</span></div>
        <div class="tr alt cols-06-17"><span>274–274a</span><span>Deferred tax recognition, asymmetric between assets and liabilities; small-company exemption</span></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 2 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s2"><span class="num">2</span><h2>Scope</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cw span2">
      <div class="ctitle cw">Every Kapitalgesellschaft (and §264a-caught partnership), scaled by size</div>
      <div class="principle">§§266–274a bind every Kapitalgesellschaften and §264a-caught partnership established in the preceding poster – but the PRECISE extent of the obligation (full granular detail versus an abbreviated format; full deferred tax recognition versus complete exemption) scales directly with the size classification §267/§267a establishes.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 3 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s3"><span class="num">3</span><h2>§266(1) – the Abbreviated Format for Small/Micro Corporations</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cd span2">
      <div class="ctitle cd">Letters and Roman numerals only – the granular arabic-numeral detail may be skipped</div>
      <div class="principle">§266(1) requires the FULL prescribed structure (Sections 4–5) from large and medium-sized corporations, but permits small and micro corporations to prepare a "verkürzte" (abbreviated) Bilanz – showing only the higher-level, LETTER-and-ROMAN-NUMERAL headings (e.g. "B. Umlaufvermögen" and its Roman-numeral sub-headings), WITHOUT the more granular arabic-numeral line items beneath them.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 4 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s4"><span class="num">4</span><h2>§266(2) – THE Prescribed Asset-Side Format</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card ci span2">
      <div class="ctitle ci">Every asset-side item from this entire poster series has a fixed home here</div>
      <div class="td-table">
        <div class="th"><span></span><span>Asset-side (Aktiva) structure</span></div>
        <div class="tr"><span>A.</span><span>Anlagevermögen – I. Immaterielle Vermögensgegenstände (incl. goodwill, §246) · II. Sachanlagen · III. Finanzanlagen</span></div>
        <div class="tr alt"><span>B.</span><span>Umlaufvermögen – I. Vorräte · II. Forderungen und sonstige Vermögensgegenstände · III. Wertpapiere · IV. Kassenbestand, Bundesbankguthaben, Guthaben bei Kreditinstituten, Schecks</span></div>
        <div class="tr"><span>C.</span><span>Rechnungsabgrenzungsposten (the named RAP category from §250)</span></div>
        <div class="tr alt"><span>D.</span><span>Aktive latente Steuern (deferred tax assets, §274)</span></div>
        <div class="tr"><span>E.</span><span>Aktiver Unterschiedsbetrag aus der Vermögensverrechnung (the §246(2) plan-asset netting difference)</span></div>
      </div>
      <div class="note">Notice: goodwill from §246(1) sentence 4, RAP from §250, and deferred tax assets from §274 all appear here, EXACTLY where this format places them – the abstract rules from earlier posters become concrete, addressable line items.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 5 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s5"><span class="num">5</span><h2>§266(3) – THE Prescribed Equity-and-Liabilities-Side Format</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cd span2">
      <div class="ctitle cd">Rückstellungen and RAP find their mirror-image home here</div>
      <div class="td-table">
        <div class="th"><span></span><span>Equity-and-liabilities-side (Passiva) structure</span></div>
        <div class="tr"><span>A.</span><span>Eigenkapital – I. Gezeichnetes Kapital · II. Kapitalrücklage · III. Gewinnrücklagen · IV. Gewinn-/Verlustvortrag · V. Jahresüberschuss/-fehlbetrag</span></div>
        <div class="tr alt"><span>B.</span><span>Rückstellungen (the mandatory, closed-list category from §249)</span></div>
        <div class="tr"><span>C.</span><span>Verbindlichkeiten (liabilities, including long-term debt from §253's discounting rules)</span></div>
        <div class="tr alt"><span>D.</span><span>Rechnungsabgrenzungsposten (RAP, liability side)</span></div>
        <div class="tr"><span>E.</span><span>Passive latente Steuern (deferred tax liabilities, §274)</span></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 6 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s6"><span class="num">6</span><h2>§267 – the Four Size Classes (2024 Thresholds, Confirmed)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cw span2">
      <div class="ctitle cw">Two of three criteria, on two consecutive balance sheet dates – thresholds raised roughly 25% in 2024</div>
      <div class="principle">A German legislative amendment effective for fiscal years beginning after 31 December 2023 raised the monetary thresholds by approximately 25%, implementing an EU-mandated inflation adjustment (Delegated Directive (EU) 2023/2775). Classification requires meeting (not exceeding) at least TWO of the three criteria on TWO CONSECUTIVE balance sheet dates.</div>
      <div class="td-table">
        <div class="th"><span>Class</span><span>Balance sheet total</span><span>Revenue</span><span>Employees</span></div>
        <div class="tr"><span>Micro (Kleinst-)</span><span>≤ €450,000</span><span>≤ €900,000</span><span>≤ 10</span></div>
        <div class="tr alt"><span>Small (klein)</span><span>≤ €7,500,000</span><span>≤ €15,000,000</span><span>≤ 50</span></div>
        <div class="tr"><span>Medium (mittelgroß)</span><span>≤ €25,000,000</span><span>≤ €50,000,000</span><span>≤ 250</span></div>
        <div class="tr alt"><span>Large (groß)</span><span>exceeds ≥2 of the medium thresholds</span><span></span><span></span></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 7 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s7"><span class="num">7</span><h2>Worked Example – Classifying a Company's Size</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cs span2">
      <div class="ctitle cs">Applying the two-of-three, two-consecutive-years test</div>
      <div class="worked">
        <div class="wk-title">Example 1: A company straddling the small/medium boundary</div>
        <div class="wk-body">A GmbH reports, for two consecutive fiscal years: Year 1 – balance sheet total €9,000,000, revenue €14,000,000, 45 employees. Year 2 – balance sheet total €9,500,000, revenue €14,500,000, 48 employees.</div>
        <div class="wk-answer">In BOTH years, the balance sheet total EXCEEDS the small-company threshold (€7,500,000), but revenue and employee count BOTH remain within the small-company limits (€15,000,000 and 50). Since only ONE of three criteria is exceeded (not two), the company remains classified as <strong>SMALL</strong> for both years – meeting at least two of three thresholds is what matters, not merely the single largest figure.</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 8 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s8"><span class="num">8</span><h2>§267a – Kleinstkapitalgesellschaften (Micro-Corporations)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cd span2">
      <div class="ctitle cd">A subset of "small," with even lighter obligations</div>
      <div class="text-supporting">§267a defines Kleinstkapitalgesellschaften as a SUBSET of small corporations meeting the even lower €450,000/€900,000/10-employee thresholds. Micro-corporations may, among other reliefs, DISPENSE with preparing a separate Anhang entirely, provided certain specific disclosures (e.g. on financial commitments and, where applicable, loans to executive bodies) are instead shown directly BENEATH the Bilanz itself.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 9 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s9"><span class="num">9</span><h2>Capital-Market-Oriented Companies Are ALWAYS "Large"</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cw span2">
      <div class="ctitle cw">Size-based relief is unavailable regardless of actual balance sheet figures</div>
      <div class="text-supporting">Regardless of its actual balance sheet total, revenue, or employee count, a company meeting the §264d "kapitalmarktorientiert" definition established in the previous poster is ALWAYS treated as a LARGE corporation for size-classification purposes – a genuinely small, capital-market-oriented company gets none of the size-based relief an equally small, non-listed company would otherwise enjoy.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 10 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s10"><span class="num">10</span><h2>§268 – Profit Appropriation: Before or After Distribution</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card ci span2">
      <div class="ctitle ci">A genuine presentation choice affecting how the bottom of equity looks</div>
      <div class="principle">§268(1) permits the Bilanz to be prepared EITHER "nach" (after) partial or full appropriation of the year's profit (i.e., already reflecting a proposed or resolved dividend distribution, so the equity section shows only the REMAINING, undistributed retained earnings) OR "vor" (before) appropriation (showing the full, undiminished Jahresüberschuss as its own separate line, with the distribution decision left entirely to a later resolution). This is a genuine PRESENTATION election, changing how the bottom of the equity section reads without changing the underlying total equity itself.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 11 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s11"><span class="num">11</span><h2>Worked Example – the Two Presentation Bases, Side by Side</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cs span2">
      <div class="ctitle cs">Same total equity, two different equity-section presentations</div>
      <div class="worked">
        <div class="wk-title">Example 2: A GmbH resolving a partial dividend before its accounts are finalised</div>
        <div class="wk-body">A GmbH earns €500,000 net income for the year. Before finalising its Jahresabschluss, its shareholders resolve to distribute €200,000 as a dividend, retaining €300,000.</div>
        <div class="wk-answer">Presented "VOR Gewinnverwendung" (before appropriation): the Bilanz shows Jahresüberschuss of the FULL <strong>€500,000</strong> as its own line, with the €200,000 distribution disclosed as a proposed appropriation (often in the Anhang) rather than already reflected. Presented "NACH Gewinnverwendung" (after appropriation): the Bilanz instead shows only the RETAINED <strong>€300,000</strong> flowing into Gewinnvortrag/Gewinnrücklagen, with the €200,000 already removed as a dividend payable. Total equity is IDENTICAL either way – only the internal presentation differs.</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 12 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s12"><span class="num">12</span><h2>§270 – Formation of the Capital and Statutory Reserves</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cd span2">
      <div class="ctitle cd">How the Kapitalrücklage and Gewinnrücklagen actually get formed</div>
      <div class="text-supporting">§270 supplies the mechanical rules for how amounts flow INTO the Kapitalrücklage (capital reserve – typically share premium and other capital contributions from shareholders) and the Gewinnrücklagen (retained earnings reserves – formed from the company's own accumulated results, or via a capital reduction, subject to any specific statutory or articles-of-association requirements).</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 13 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s13"><span class="num">13</span><h2>§271 – Beteiligungen and Verbundene Unternehmen (Definitions That Ripple Outward)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cw span2">
      <div class="ctitle cw">Two definitions used repeatedly throughout the rest of Book 3</div>
      <div class="text-supporting">§271(1) defines a "Beteiligung" (participating interest) as a shareholding intended to serve the holder's OWN business through a durable connection – with a REBUTTABLE PRESUMPTION that any holding of 20% or more of the nominal capital qualifies. §271(2) defines "verbundene Unternehmen" (affiliated companies) – entities linked through the consolidation-relevant control and ownership relationships the LATER Konzernabschluss Titel elaborates in full. These two definitions are used repeatedly throughout the REST of Book 3, wherever a disclosure or presentation rule refers to a "Beteiligung" or an "verbundenes Unternehmen" without redefining the term afresh.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 14 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s14"><span class="num">14</span><h2>§272(1) – Treasury Shares: a Rare, Clean Point of IFRS Convergence</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cd span2">
      <div class="ctitle cd">Deducted directly from equity – exactly as IAS 32 requires</div>
      <div class="principle">§272(1) requires gezeichnetes Kapital (subscribed/share capital) to be shown at its FULL nominal amount even where not fully paid in – but ALSO requires treasury shares (eigene Anteile – a company's own shares it has reacquired) to be DEDUCTED directly from equity, rather than shown as an asset. This is a genuinely CLEAN point of convergence with IFRS: IAS 32 likewise prohibits recognising treasury shares as an asset, requiring their deduction from equity instead – a rare instance in this poster series of the two frameworks landing on precisely the SAME accounting outcome, for essentially the SAME conceptual reason (a company cannot meaningfully hold an "asset" interest in its own equity).</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 15 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s15"><span class="num">15</span><h2>Worked Example – Deducting Treasury Shares From Equity</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card ci span2">
      <div class="ctitle ci">Reducing equity directly, never recognising an asset</div>
      <div class="worked">
        <div class="wk-title">Example 3: An AG repurchasing its own shares</div>
        <div class="wk-body">An AG repurchases €800,000 of its own shares on the open market, intending to hold them for a possible future employee share scheme.</div>
        <div class="wk-answer">The AG does <strong>NOT</strong> recognise an €800,000 asset for the repurchased shares. Instead, it reduces total EQUITY directly by €800,000 – presented as a deduction within the equity section – CONSISTENT with both §272(1) and, independently, with IAS 32's own identical treatment internationally.</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 16 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s16"><span class="num">16</span><h2>§272(2)–(4) – Capital Reserve, Retained Earnings, and the Parent-Shares Reserve</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cw span2">
      <div class="ctitle cw">A distinctive, specifically named reserve for a subsidiary holding shares in its own parent</div>
      <div class="check-list">
        <span>§272(2): the Kapitalrücklage's composition – share premium (Agio) and other specific capital contributions from shareholders</span>
        <span>§272(3): Gewinnrücklagen may be formed only from current or prior years' results, or via a capital reduction, per statute or the articles of association</span>
        <span>§272(4): a distinctively NAMED "Rücklage für Anteile an einem herrschenden oder mehrheitlich beteiligten Unternehmen" – a specific reserve required where a subsidiary holds shares in its OWN controlling or majority-owning parent, restricting distributable reserves to the extent of that holding's value</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 17 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s17"><span class="num">17</span><h2>§274 – Deferred Taxes: an Asymmetric Recognition Rule</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cd span2">
      <div class="ctitle cd">Mandatory for a net liability position; ELECTIVE for a net asset position</div>
      <div class="principle">§274(1) requires deferred taxes to be recognised for TEMPORARY DIFFERENCES between the commercial (Handelsbilanz) carrying amounts of assets, liabilities, and RAP items and their corresponding TAX bases – directly connecting back to the Maßgeblichkeitsprinzip discussed in the earlier §§242–245 poster. Critically, and consistent with the ASYMMETRIC prudence running throughout this entire series: where the OVERALL, NET effect across all temporary differences is a deferred tax LIABILITY, recognition is MANDATORY. Where the overall NET effect is instead a deferred tax ASSET, recognition remains merely an ELECTION (Aktivierungswahlrecht) – an enterprise MAY recognise it, but need not.</div>
      <div class="callout warn">This asymmetric treatment mirrors precisely the same Vorsichts-driven pattern we have now seen repeatedly across this series – mandatory recognition for items that WORSEN the balance sheet picture, elective recognition for items that would IMPROVE it.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 18 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s18"><span class="num">18</span><h2>Worked Example – a Mandatory Deferred Tax Liability</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cs span2">
      <div class="ctitle cs">A net liability position, with no election available</div>
      <div class="worked">
        <div class="wk-title">Example 4: Accelerated tax depreciation creating a net taxable temporary difference</div>
        <div class="wk-body">A company's tax accounts permit accelerated depreciation on certain equipment, producing a lower tax carrying amount than the commercial (Handelsbilanz) carrying amount. Across all its temporary differences, the NET effect is a future tax OUTFLOW of €140,000 at the company's applicable 30% tax rate.</div>
        <div class="wk-answer">Being a NET deferred tax LIABILITY position, the company MUST recognise a €140,000 × 30% = <strong>€42,000</strong> deferred tax liability – this is not elective, unlike the equivalent asset-side scenario in the next example.</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 19 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s19"><span class="num">19</span><h2>Worked Example – an Elective Deferred Tax Asset</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card ci span2">
      <div class="ctitle ci">A net asset position, where recognition remains the company's choice</div>
      <div class="worked">
        <div class="wk-title">Example 5: Tax loss carryforwards and provisions creating a net deductible position</div>
        <div class="wk-body">The same company also has significant Rückstellungen recognised for commercial purposes that are not yet tax-deductible, plus available tax loss carryforwards – such that, across ALL its temporary differences, the overall NET effect is a future tax BENEFIT of €90,000.</div>
        <div class="wk-answer">Being a NET deferred tax ASSET position, the company MAY elect to recognise €90,000 as a deferred tax asset, but is NOT required to – many companies, particularly smaller ones wary of the ongoing recoverability assessment this election entails, simply choose NOT to recognise it, leaving the potential future benefit entirely off the Bilanz.</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 20 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s20"><span class="num">20</span><h2>§274a – Small Corporations Are Exempt From §274 Entirely</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cw span2">
      <div class="ctitle cw">A complete opt-out from deferred tax accounting for smaller enterprises</div>
      <div class="text-supporting">§274a exempts SMALL corporations (per §267's thresholds) from applying §274's deferred tax provisions altogether – a complete opt-out, not merely a simplified version, reflecting the same cost-benefit reasoning behind virtually every size-dependent relief this series has documented: the administrative cost of tracking temporary differences and applying deferred tax accounting is judged disproportionate to its benefit for smaller enterprises' typical user base.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 21 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s21"><span class="num">21</span><h2>Critical Judgments &amp; Practical Difficulties</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cn span2">
      <div class="ctitle cn">Where judgement matters most</div>
      <div class="two-col">
        <div>
          <div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT JUDGEMENTS</div>
          <div class="dot-list">
            <span>Whether the overall, net deferred tax position genuinely nets to a liability (mandatory) or asset (elective) once ALL temporary differences are aggregated</span>
            <span>Whether a shareholding rebuts or confirms the 20% Beteiligung presumption under §271(1)</span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div>
          <div class="elabel">PRACTICAL DIFFICULTIES</div>
          <div class="dot-list">
            <span>Tracking a company's size classification across the required two consecutive balance sheet dates, particularly near a threshold boundary</span>
            <span>Choosing consistently between the "vor" and "nach Gewinnverwendung" presentation bases, and applying that choice from year to year per §265(1)'s consistency principle</span>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 22 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s22"><span class="num">22</span><h2>Relationship to IFRS (IAS 1, IAS 12, IAS 32)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card ci span2">
      <div class="ctitle ci">Comparison – HGB §§266–274a vs IFRS</div>
      <div class="td-table">
        <div class="th cols-12-1-1"><span>Feature</span><span>HGB</span><span>IFRS</span></div>
        <div class="tr cols-12-1-1"><span>Balance sheet format</span><span>LEGALLY PRESCRIBED, line-by-line (§266)</span><span>Illustrative only – IAS 1 sets minimum line items, not a rigid format</span></div>
        <div class="tr alt cols-12-1-1"><span>Treasury shares</span><span>Deducted from equity (§272(1))</span><span>Deducted from equity (IAS 32) – CONVERGED</span></div>
        <div class="tr cols-12-1-1"><span>Deferred tax asset recognition</span><span>ELECTIVE, where net position is an asset</span><span>MANDATORY, subject to a recoverability test (IAS 12)</span></div>
        <div class="tr alt cols-12-1-1"><span>Deferred tax liability recognition</span><span>MANDATORY</span><span>MANDATORY – broadly converged in this direction</span></div>
      </div>
      <div class="note">The prescribed, rigid FORMAT itself is arguably the single most visible day-to-day difference a reader moving between HGB and IFRS statements will notice – IFRS's minimum-line-item approach under IAS 1 leaves considerably more presentation flexibility than HGB's exhaustively legislated schema.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 23 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s23"><span class="num">23</span><h2>Statement Mapping</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cs span2">
      <div class="ctitle cs">Where every §§266–274a event lands</div>
      <div class="td-table">
        <div class="th cols-16-1-14"><span>Event / obligation</span><span>Statement(s) affected</span><span>Notes</span></div>
        <div class="tr cols-16-1-14"><span>Treasury share repurchase</span><span><span class="badge bs">Bilanz</span></span><span>Direct equity reduction, no asset recognised</span></div>
        <div class="tr alt cols-16-1-14"><span>Mandatory deferred tax liability</span><span><span class="badge bs">Bilanz</span> <span class="badge bp">GuV</span></span><span>Recognised regardless of size, unless §274a small-company exemption applies</span></div>
        <div class="tr cols-16-1-14"><span>Profit appropriation presentation choice</span><span><span class="badge bs">Bilanz</span></span><span>Affects presentation only, not total equity</span></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 24 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s24"><span class="num">24</span><h2>Related Provisions – Cross-Reference Map</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="grid">
    <div class="card cd span2">
      <div class="ctitle cd">How §§266–274a connect across HGB Book 3</div>
      <div class="td-table">
        <div class="th cols-08-2"><span>Provision</span><span>Nature of the connection</span></div>
        <div class="tr cols-08-2"><span>§§246, §249, §250</span><span>Goodwill, Rückstellungen, and RAP all populate the exact line items §266 prescribes here</span></div>
        <div class="tr alt cols-08-2"><span>§264d</span><span>Supplies the capital-market-oriented definition that overrides §267's size-based classification entirely</span></div>
        <div class="tr cols-08-2"><span>§275</span><span>The parallel prescribed format for the GuV, structured on the same general principles §265 establishes</span></div>
        <div class="tr alt cols-08-2"><span>§290 et seq.</span><span>Relies on §271(2)'s verbundene Unternehmen definition for its own consolidation scope tests</span></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ============ SECTION 25 ============ -->
  <div class="section-header" id="s25"><span class="num">25</span><h2>Quick-Reference Glossary</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
  <div class="three-col">
    <div class="card cn">
      <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Beteiligung</div><div class="glossary-def">A participating interest – a shareholding intended to serve a durable connection with one's own business, presumed at 20%+ ownership.</div></div>
      <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Verbundene Unternehmen</div><div class="glossary-def">Affiliated companies, linked through consolidation-relevant control and ownership relationships.</div></div>
    </div>
    <div class="card cn">
      <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Eigene Anteile</div><div class="glossary-def">Treasury shares – a company's own reacquired shares, deducted directly from equity rather than recognised as an asset.</div></div>
      <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Gewinnverwendung</div><div class="glossary-def">Profit appropriation – the decision to distribute or retain a year's net income, presentable either before or after in the Bilanz.</div></div>
    </div>
    <div class="card cn">
      <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Aktivierungswahlrecht</div><div class="glossary-def">A capitalisation election – here, the optional (not mandatory) recognition of a net deferred tax asset position.</div></div>
      <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Kleinstkapitalgesellschaft</div><div class="glossary-def">Micro-corporation – the smallest size class, permitted to dispense with a separate Anhang under specific conditions.</div></div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="legend">
    <div class="ltitle">Key</div>
    <div class="lrow">
      <span class="badge bs">Bilanz</span> Balance sheet
      <span class="badge bp">GuV</span> Income statement (Gewinn- und Verlustrechnung)
      <span class="badge be">Anhang</span> Notes to the financial statements
    </div>
  </div>
